2-domain-design.md:13:The first util is the **durable work queue**. Some of what a domain owns cannot run in the hot path: smoothing a closed turn, summarizing a chunk, rebuilding derivations after an edit. The owning domain records that work as a durable work item and moves on; a worker picks it up later and runs the domain's handler for it. Two properties carry the design. Pending work is recorded durably, not held in memory, so a restart loses nothing. And a thread's queued work is ordered: work queued by an edit lands after work already in flight on the old content, so a rebuilt derivation is never overwritten by a straggler finishing late. Running one item at a time per thread is the current policy that provides this, and the simplest. Independent items could run in parallel later, but only in a way that preserves the ordering edits lean on.\ndocs/onboard/02-domain-design.md:15:Each kind of queued work has exactly one owning domain. A domain queues its own work; it never queues work into another domain, and no domain watches for another domain's items. When a flow crosses domains, the crossing is a surface call, and the called domain queues whatever work its part needs. Asking a domain to repair its own derivations works the same way: the ask is a surface call, and the owning domain decides what to queue. The queue owns the mechanics of an item: recorded, picked up, retried, finished. What the work means belongs entirely to the owning domain.\ndocs/onboard/02-domain-design.md:88:As a harness runs it produces a stream of events. An event is one recorded thing that happened in the thread:\ndocs/onboard/02-domain-design.md:141:Events carry an idempotency key from the harness. If an event with the same key was already recorded for the thread, it is skipped rather than written twice, so a harness that resends a batch after a failure does not produce duplicates.\ndocs/onboard/02-domain-design.md:145:Turn boundaries are decided in the hot path, as events land, while intake is the thing watching the ordered stream. `intake-stream` calls the `turns` surface to apply the turn state machine and get the turn membership for message-producing events. This keeps membership correct: a message is attached to its turn when it is recorded, not inferred later against a stream that has moved on.\ndocs/onboard/02-domain-design.md:167:The result reports, per event, whether it was recorded, skipped as a duplicate, or rejected. A rejected event stops the batch, and events already written in the same transaction are rolled back, so a batch either lands cleanly or not at all. The result also reports turn boundaries that the batch opened or closed.\ndocs/onboard/02-domain-design.md:186:  I->>M: create message from recorded event\ndocs/onboard/02-domain-design.md:255:The open or close decision is synchronous. The result tells intake which turn, if any, a message-producing event belongs to. This is how messages get attached to a turn while the stream is still being recorded.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:9:**What gets mocked: nothing.** This epic has no network, no inference provider, no clock-sensitive contract beyond injected `clock`, and its filesystem behavior *is* the product (durability, atomicity, restart survival). Every test runs against real SQLite files in a per-test temp directory. The injected clock is fixed per test for deterministic `recordedAt`/`queuedAt`/`createdAt`; the tokenizer runs real because golden-counting real output is stronger than stubbing it.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:34:| `threadWith(events)` | created thread + recorded batch via real SDK | post-Chunk-2: real pipeline output |\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:76:| TC-3.4 | orphan turn_end | turn_end as first event | event recorded order 1; zero turns; zero work; next prompt opens t1 |\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:88:| TC-4.2 | first-failure index | valid, valid, invalid at index 2 | `eventIndex: 2`; events 0–1 not recorded |\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:99:| TC-5.1 | full resend | record 5, resend same 5 | zero recorded; five skips with `duplicate_idempotency_key`; read-back unchanged |\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:100:| TC-5.2 | partial resend | 3 recorded + 2 new | three skips, two recorded; orders continue dense (no gaps from skips) |\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:102:| TC-5.4 | skips inert | resend recorded prompt + turn_end mid-conversation | turn count/states unchanged; no transitions in result; work-item count unchanged; no duplicate message |\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:103:| TC-5.5 | key wins | key K with payload A recorded; resend K with valid payload B | skipped; read-back returns A; B nowhere in any table (`openRaw` scan) |\npackages/lhc/src/shared/context.ts:7:  clock: () => Date; // injected for deterministic recordedAt/queuedAt in tests\npackages/lhc/test/lifecycle.test.ts:11:// surfaces stamp into outputs (event recorded_at, view created_at, mutation\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:1:// Recorded-corpus loader (tech design §Fixture Contracts). Each corpus is a\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:2:// named lifecycle paired with the LHC intake events it should produce. Until M0\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:23:    name: \"chatty\",\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:44:    name: \"tool-heavy\",\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:71:    name: \"parallel-tool\",\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:99:    name: \"error-result\",\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:120:    name: \"aborted-turn\",\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:138:  chatty: loadChattyCorpus,\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:139:  \"tool-heavy\": loadToolHeavyCorpus,\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:140:  \"parallel-tool\": loadParallelToolCorpus,\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:141:  \"error-result\": loadErrorResultCorpus,\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:142:  \"aborted-turn\": loadAbortedTurnCorpus,\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:181:/** Check a corpus yields well-formed `MessageEventInput` shapes and a\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:185:export function validateCorpus(corpus: Corpus): CorpusValidation {\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:187:  if (corpus.name.trim() === \"\") problems.push(\"corpus has an empty name\");\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:188:  if (corpus.expected.length === 0) problems.push(`${corpus.name}: no expected events`);\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:191:  for (const [i, ev] of corpus.expected.entries()) {\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:192:    const at = `${corpus.name}[${i}]`;\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:235:  const last = corpus.expected[corpus.expected.length - 1];\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:237:    problems.push(`${corpus.name}: sequence does not close with turn_end`);\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:17:- **Event**: one unit of harness activity (a prompt, a piece of assistant output, a tool call or result, a runtime note, a turn-end marker), recorded in stream order.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:26:A harness integration creates a thread, then streams conversation activity into it as event batches. Each batch lands atomically: events are recorded in order, messages and blocks are projected with token estimates, turn state advances through a small fixed rule set, and derivation work queues durably — all synchronously, before the call returns. Resends are safe through per-event idempotency keys. Incoherent batches are rejected whole, leaving the thread untouched.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:28:Everything in this epic is deterministic and local. No inference runs anywhere in these flows; queued derivation work is recorded, not executed.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:47:**Operators** come later (inspection is Epic 04), but everything recorded here is what they will audit. Nothing in this epic may record state that cannot be read back and explained.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:130:Event kinds: `user_prompt`, `assistant_text`, `assistant_thinking`, `tool_call`, `tool_result`, `runtime_note`, `turn_end`. All except `turn_end` produce a message; `turn_end` is recorded as an event only.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:132:The envelope and event objects are strict contracts: an unknown field anywhere, on the envelope, on an event, or in a payload, is a validation failure unless this contract explicitly lists it. Nothing is silently dropped. Validation precedes idempotency: every event in the batch must pass shape validation before any skip decision is made. A duplicate key on a malformed event is a rejection, not a skip. Tool-result payloads are recorded in full at intake; any truncation or summarization of tool output is derived or rendering behavior belonging to later epics, never a property of the record.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:136:- **AC-2.1**: A batch of valid events is recorded in array order, with event order continuing from the thread's last recorded event; a following batch continues the same sequence.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:138:- **AC-2.3**: A `turn_end` event is recorded in the event order but produces no message.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:140:- **AC-2.5**: A `tool_result` payload is recorded and read back in full, regardless of size; intake never truncates or summarizes record content.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:141:- **AC-2.6**: Each event's actor and harness identifiers are recorded as given and carried onto its message.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:142:- **AC-2.7**: The batch result reports, per event: recorded or skipped, and for recorded message-producing events the created message id; plus any turn transitions the batch caused and any work items it queued.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:144:- **AC-2.9**: An empty batch is a caller error; nothing is recorded.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:169:| 0 | `turn_end` | No turn effect; the event is still recorded |\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:181:- **AC-3.5**: A `turn_end` arriving with no open turn has no turn effect and is still recorded as an event.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:192:- **TC-3.4** (AC-3.5): `turn_end` as a thread's first event → recorded, no turn exists, no work queued, subsequent prompt opens turn 1 normally.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:195:- **TC-3.7** (AC-3.9): Manufacture a two-open-turns state in a fixture thread → any batch fails with the corruption error class; read-back confirms nothing recorded.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:209:- Any caller-supplied server-generated field: event order, recorded-at time, or a server id\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:234:A harness that crashed or timed out mid-send cannot know what landed. It resends the same batch. Every event carries a caller-chosen idempotency key, unique per thread; events whose keys are already recorded are skipped, the rest land normally, and the result says which was which.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:236:Deduplication is by key alone. Content under a reused key is not compared; the key is the harness's claim that this is the same event. Skips happen only after the whole batch passes validation: a malformed event is a rejection even when its key is already recorded.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:240:- **AC-5.1**: Resending a fully recorded batch records nothing, skips every event, and reports each skip with its idempotency key.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:241:- **AC-5.2**: Resending a batch where some events are recorded and some are new skips the recorded ones and records the new ones in batch order.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:244:- **AC-5.5**: A valid event reusing a recorded key with different content is skipped; the original record is unchanged and the new content is not stored anywhere.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:248:- **TC-5.1** (AC-5.1): Record a five-event batch, resend it identically → zero recorded, five skips reported by key, each carrying `skipReason: duplicate_idempotency_key`; full read-back unchanged.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:249:- **TC-5.2** (AC-5.2): Resend with three old events plus two new → three skips, two recorded continuing the event order.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:251:- **TC-5.4** (AC-5.4): A resent batch containing a recorded `user_prompt` and a recorded `turn_end` → turn count and turn states unchanged; work-item count unchanged.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:252:- **TC-5.5** (AC-5.5): Key K recorded with payload A; resend key K with valid payload B → skipped, read-back still returns payload A, no new message or work item.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:304:| `actor` | string | yes | Non-empty; recorded as given (for example `user`, `assistant`) |\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:324:Rejected if present on any event: event order, recorded-at, any server-generated id, schema version. These are produced by the system, never accepted from the caller.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:330:| `events` | array | Per input event, in order: `{ idempotencyKey, outcome: recorded \\| skipped, messageId?, skipReason? }`. `skipReason` present exactly when skipped; sole value in this epic: `duplicate_idempotency_key` |\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:361:- **Event**: `{ eventOrder, eventKind, idempotencyKey, actor, harness, payload, recordedAt }`\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:376:- Batch intake is synchronous: when the call returns, everything it reports (events, messages, turn transitions, queued work) is durably recorded.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:386:3. What tokenizer provides the base-unit estimate, and how is its identity recorded so estimates remain interpretable if the base ever changes? (Tech arch names the intended choice; design confirms and pins it.)\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:428:**Delivers:** Messages and blocks projected from recorded events in the same operation, token estimates stamped, actor/harness carried, message read-back.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:429:**Governing idea:** Everything recorded becomes readable, with its size known.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:431:**Boundary / risk notes:** Projection extends the existing atomic operation — a projection failure must reject the batch, not strand recorded events without messages.\ndocs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:447:**Governing idea:** All derivation work caused by an accepted batch is durably recorded by the time the batch's call returns.\npackages/lhc/test/report-repair.test.ts:409:    // No auto-cascade: the rendering still carries its recorded gap.\npackages/lhc/test/epic-fix-02.test.ts:5:// whose manifest hash was re-recorded to bless that one change).\npackages/lhc/reference/src-green-thread-events-v1/thread-events/schema.ts:254:  recordedAt: Schema.String,\npackages/lhc/reference/src-green-thread-events-v1/thread-events/schema.ts:336:  const generatedFields = [\"schemaVersion\", \"threadEventId\", \"threadId\", \"eventOrder\", \"recordedAt\"].filter((field) => field in value);\npackages/pi-lhc/test/lifecycle/instance.test.ts:118:    // the lifecycle proof needs only that recorded events survive shutdown).\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:56:      \"recorded\",\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:57:      \"recorded\",\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:58:      \"recorded\",\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:95:    // recordedAt is sourced from the injected clock (test-plan: fixed per\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:97:    // read-back can be compared field-for-field — recordedAt included, nothing\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:99:    const recordedAt = new Date(\"2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z\");\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:100:    setIntakeClock(() => recordedAt);\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:123:    // recordedAt included. Determinism comes from the injected clock, not from\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:134:    // recordedAt is the injected instant, proving it came from the clock seam\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:136:    expect(readById.value.map((e: EventRecord) => e.recordedAt)).toEqual(\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:137:      readByPath.map(() => recordedAt.toISOString()),\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:144:    const recorded = await intakeStream.messageEvents({ filePath }, baselineBatch);\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:145:    expect(recorded.ok).toBe(true);\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:150:    // the first event of the new batch has been recorded.\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:196:    const recorded = await intakeStream.messageEvents({ filePath }, batch);\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:197:    expect(recorded.ok).toBe(true);\npackages/lhc/reference/src-green-thread-events-v1/thread-events/persistence/triggers.ts:20:    createdAt: event.recordedAt,\npackages/lhc/test/idempotency.test.ts:47:  it(\"TC-5.1: resending a fully